+## Two ways to get the token — pick one
 
-Google will not let an agent authorise itself, and the sandbox has no browser.
-So this is done once, by hand, and the token is then reused forever.
+### Preferred: grant on your own machine (loopback)
+
+The sandbox hostname changes every time the pod is recreated, so a redirect URI
+pointing at it has to be re-registered constantly. A **loopback** redirect never
+changes: register it once, and every future pod works.
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/yt_auth.py --loopback --slug dharma
+```
+
+1. Register `http://127.0.0.1:8080/oauth2callback` **once** in Google Cloud
+   console → APIs & Services → Credentials → your OAuth client → Authorised
+   redirect URIs. You never touch this again.
+2. The command prints an authorisation URL. Open it **on your own machine**,
+   choose the Google account that owns the channel, and approve. The browser
+   lands on your loopback address and the script catches the code.
+3. The token is written to `~/.config/content-lab/yt_token_<slug>.json` (mode
+   600). Copy that file back into the sandbox if the authorisation ran locally;
+   the token file is the only thing that has to travel.
+4. Verify: `python3 scripts/yt_auth.py --check --slug dharma`.
+
+### Fallback: grant through the sandbox hostname
+
+If you cannot run the browser on your own machine, the sandbox callback still
+works — it just has to be re-registered whenever the pod changes.
 
 ```bash
-python3 scripts/yt_auth.py --print-redirects   # which URI to register
-python3 scripts/yt_auth.py                     # prints a URL, then waits
+python3 scripts/yt_auth.py --print-redirects   # which URIs to register, and why
+python3 scripts/yt_auth.py --slug dharma       # prints a URL, then waits
 ```
 
+`--print-redirects` names the source of each URI: `$RUNTIME_URL` is the current
+pod, the rest are a fallback list. If `$RUNTIME_URL` is set but missing from the
+printed list, the command exits with an error rather than silently printing a
+stale host.
+
+### When the token is already in the environment
+
+An agent session that carries `YT_CH<N>_REFRESH_TOKEN` (or
+`YT_<SLUG>_REFRESH_TOKEN`, or a `YT_CHANNEL_TOKENS` JSON blob) plus
+`YT_CLIENT_ID` / `YT_CLIENT_SECRET` needs **no human and no browser at all**.
+The first `--check` or upload writes that refresh token into the token file and
+carries on. When several tokens are present, the one belonging to the channel
+being published is chosen by asking Google which channel each token owns — so a
+four-channel secret is self-selecting and a token for the wrong channel is never
+used. This is why "the AI cannot post" is almost always a missing token file
+rather than a missing grant.
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/yt_auth.py --check --slug dharma --expected-channel UCM3QO5L_pmj34n1JVlbhh-w
+```
+
